  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "American financier Sanford Weill sought to connect school lessons with working life through the National Academy Foundation. Teachers spent summers in businesses, while industry professionals helped develop relevant courses. Students received internships intended to show them careers beyond entry-level employment and the education those careers required.\n\nHe also encouraged collaboration between medical researchers and university scientists. To help teams from Berkeley and UCSF get acquainted, he hosted a square dance at his Sonoma home. He believed mistakes on the dance floor made people meet and work together while pursuing a joint programme in neuroscience.",
    "html": "<p>American financier Sanford Weill sought to connect school lessons with working life through the National Academy Foundation. Teachers spent summers in businesses, while industry professionals helped develop relevant courses. Students received internships intended to show them careers beyond entry-level employment and the education those careers required.</p>\r\n<p>He also encouraged collaboration between medical researchers and university scientists. To help teams from Berkeley and UCSF get acquainted, he hosted a square dance at his Sonoma home. He believed mistakes on the dance floor made people meet and work together while pursuing a joint programme in neuroscience.</p>\r\n",
